Every value is between `1` and `n`, so each value can point to an array position. The code changes the sign at that position to mark it as visited. If the position is already negative, the current value has appeared before.

The input array is modified. That is the main trade-off, although the method uses constant extra space. A duplicate such as `[2, 2]` is handled on the second visit to index `2`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        for (int value : nums) {
            int index = Math.abs(value);

            if (nums[index] < 0) {
                return index;
            }

            nums[index] = -nums[index];
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, not counting the input array. The input array is changed.

This binary search is over possible values, not array positions. For a middle value, count how many numbers are less than or equal to it. If that count is bigger than `mid`, the duplicate must be in the lower half. Otherwise, search the upper half.

The array stays unchanged. The cost is a little higher than the linear constant-space method because every binary search step scans the whole array.

Dry run for `[1, 3, 4, 2, 2]`: with values `1..4`, `mid` is `2`. There are three values at most `2`, which is more than `2`, so the answer is in `1..2`. The next checks lead to `2`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        int low = 1;
        int high = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            int count = 0;

            for (int value : nums) {
                if (value <= mid) {
                    ++count;
                }
            }

            if (count > mid) {
                high = mid;
            } else {
                low = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        return low;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is not modified.

Sorting puts equal values next to each other. After that, one pass is enough to compare each value with the one before it.

This is probably the shortest version to verify, but sorting changes `nums` and takes `O(n log n)` time. The problem guarantees a duplicate, so the final `-1` is only a fallback.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int findDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; ++i) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                return nums[i];
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the sorting stack in the usual Java implementation. The input array is modified.

## Approach
The idea is to count the frequency of every number using a hash (frequency) array.

1. Find the maximum element in the array to determine the required size of the hash array.
2. Create a frequency array (`hash`) of size `maxElement + 1` initialized with `0`.
3. Traverse the input array and increment the frequency of each element.
4. Traverse the frequency array.
   If any element has a frequency greater than `1`, that index represents the duplicate number.
5. Return the duplicate element.

